{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The stuff of the visible universe - stars, \nplanets, people - are a cosmic afterthought.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6660,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just luminous sprinkling on top of the \nvast oceans of dark matter that dominate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational universe. Although we \ndon't know what dark matter actually is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a long time we thought we at least had it's \nbehavior nailed. The so-called cold dark matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22680,
    "dDurationMs": 6129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model had to be right because it worked so well \nin explaining structure in the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28809,
    "dDurationMs": 7071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that is until it didn't. Distressing disagreements with newer observations sent many physicists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35880,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to the blackboard. But even more recently, a new generation of sophisticated, high-power computer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42420,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simulations may save our favorite dark matter \nmodel after all. With some unexpected help - it turns out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50040,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that visible matter may be much more important \nin shaping the universe than we ever imagined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61200,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have no idea what dark matter is, other than \nit’s some source of gravity that is completely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66300,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invisible but exerts way more pull that all of \nthe regular matter. More than all of the stars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72540,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of the gas, all of the black holes…unless dark \nmatter is black holes, then black holes are most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77760,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of everything. Dark matter constitutes \n80% or so of the mass in the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82200,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means even our Milky Way galaxy is mostly \na vast ball of dark matter that happens to have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88020,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attracted a relative sprinkling of \nbaryons—atoms in the form of gas,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which lit up as starry glitter spinning in the \nmiddle of this invisible gravitational well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98520,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for all of dark matter’s mysteriousness, \nit’s remarkably simple stuff in terms of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104160,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behavior. Or at least so we have long thought. \nThe mainstream model for dark matter is called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108780,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cold dark matter, or CDM. This type of dark matter \nis described as a fluid of particles that don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114780,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact with each other or anything else except \nby gravity, and that have pretty low speeds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120900,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making them cold. If a universe starts out full \nof such a fluid, any tiny lumps in density of that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128460,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluid tend to attract more dark matter and grow. \nWe can now do these incredible supercomputer simulations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135420,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this process, and based on the CDM model, \nthis type of dark matter seems to lead to exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the types of giant structures—galaxies, galaxy \nclusters, etc—that we see in the universe today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147840,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although we don’t know what this “CDM” might be \nmade of, there are some seemingly very plausible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154500,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "types of particle that could do the job. Broadly, \nwe have weakly interacting massive particles or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "WIMPs, which are predicted by various extensions \nto the standard model of particle physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165360,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or there's another exotic particle, the axion, or even the frozen Planck-scale relics of evaporated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes all behave like cold dark matter, \nand we’ve talked about all of these before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177840,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that there are many fairly natural ways \nto make cold dark matter is a point in favour of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182940,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the idea. But another one is the so-called \nWIMP miracle. Most elementary particles in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190380,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe were created soon after the Big \nBang during a short period when the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195060,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was hot enough for matter-antimatter \npairs to be created spontaneously from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199740,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the extremely energetic radiation of that \ntime. That creation process stopped when the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205440,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe expanded and cooled, and most of this \nstuff annihilated with itself. Regular matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212040,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is much more able to interact with itself, \nalmost completely annihilated so that the matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218880,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that was left is just due to the 1-in-a billion \noverabundance of normal matter over antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225180,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But dark matter particles in the WIMP category wouldn’t have self-annihilated so quickly due to its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232560,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weakly-interacting nature. Instead, it stopped \nannihilating only when the universe just got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237780,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too big for dark matter particles to find each \nother. It turns out that if you calculate how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244140,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much dark matter should have been left when \nthat annihilation stops you get pretty much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249300,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the right number for the amount of dark matter \nwe see in the universe today. That’s the WIMP  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255420,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "miracle. Now this does require the existence of \nsome unknown heavy non-standard-model particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261180,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like those predicted by supersymmetry, but \nactually the WIMP miracle is not tied to any particular theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267240,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long story short, CDM was looking great. \nBut then, starting around 20 years ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273840,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we began to notice that our rapidly \nimproving simulations and observations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278700,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe stopped matching up quite so \nwell. Let’s start on the simulation side.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285840,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because cold dark matter is cold, it should be \nable to form pretty small blobs with relatively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291540,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little gravity holding them together. That’s \nbecause cold means slow-moving particles, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297780,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have trouble escaping even weak gravitational \nfields. So if we try to simulate, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303120,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the formation of the Milky Way with this type of \ndark matter we get something weird. We get a big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter halo that attracts the gas that forms \nthe stars of the main galaxy—that part is fine.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315180,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But within this halo these CDM simulations also \npredict a lot of sub-haloes - smaller dents in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324420,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big gravitational well that themselves should \npresumably attract gas and form sort of mini  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330900,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies - what we call satellite galaxies. \nCDM simulations predict the Milky Way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337020,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and really any big galaxy should be orbited by \nthousands of these things. But our observations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343620,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of real galaxies don’t find anywhere near enough \nof them. This is the “missing satellites” problem,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349500,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it was a clue that our CDM model \nmight not be as miraculous as we once thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355980,
    "dDurationMs": 5686,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another prediction of CDM is about the shape of big galaxies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361666,
    "dDurationMs": 7451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gas in a room pushes on itself due to the fact that the particles interact creating an outer pressure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369117,
    "dDurationMs": 7263,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the weakly interacting CDM particles don’t push each other apart. Add the fact that they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376380,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving very slowly, and it becomes possible for these particles to form very dense, tight clusters. CDM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models predict that inside galaxies, the density \nof dark matter should go up and up as you travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389040,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the outskirts inwards, and should reach \na very high density cusp near the very center.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396120,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And regular matter should fall towards this \ndark matter concentration, resulting in lots  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401220,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stars near the galactic center. But in \nreal galaxies, the density usually flattens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406380,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out within several thousand light years of the \ncenter, leaving a more rounded core. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411480,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known as the “cusp-core” problem, and it’s \nthe second strike against cold dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417300,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, physics thrives on discrepancy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these problems have inspired many years of \nwork on alternatives to the standard CDM model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426600,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most obvious fix to cold dark matter is to \nchange the temperature—change the “cold” part.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433620,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t heat up dark matter too much \nor its particles won’t form halos at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all—the particles will just wizz randomly through \nthe universe and be pretty useless. But what if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442560,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we increase temperature just a bit to give us warm dark matter, or WDM? There is a way to do this, sterile neutrinos could give  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451080,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us a WDM, and we’ve discussed previously. However this neutrino abundance doesn’t “miraculously” come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out just right from the particle physics and \nthe cosmology in the way that WIMPs seem to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464400,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If dark matter is made of a relatively light \nparticle that has a slightly higher “temperature”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470120,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they can still form big haloes but have \na harder time forming these subhaloes. That’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476820,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply because small haloes don’t have strong \nenough gravitational fields to hold these faster  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481620,
    "dDurationMs": 5464,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving particles. So maybe that's why the \nMilky Way has very few satellite galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487084,
    "dDurationMs": 6228,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This doesn't really help us with the \ncusp-core problem, but here's a proposal that may. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493312,
    "dDurationMs": 6188,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Self-interacting dark matter (SIDM) proposes \nthat dark matter particles actually do interact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499500,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with themselves a bit more than standard CDM \nwould assume. That means they can repel and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scatter off each other, which stops them becoming \npacked too tightly above a certain density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509760,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, there’s also fuzzy dark matter (FDM), \nwhich we talked about in our recent episode.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516300,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this model, dark matter is a superfluid of \nultra-light axions with a de Broglie wavelength  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521760,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousands of light years long. It can’t form \nstructures smaller than this wavelength,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526980,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so may neatly eliminate tiny satellites \nand may eliminate very concentrated galactic cores,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533280,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps solving both the “missing \nsatellites” and “cusp-core” problems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536940,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have a few reasonable solutions \nto our dark matter woes. But which is right?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543540,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, not so fast. While all of this new physics \nwas being concocted, there was a parallel effort  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551340,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see if the problems with cold dark matter could \nbe fixed while keeping dark matter cold. Remember,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557100,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CDM is appealing as a model for more reasons than \nits behavior as dark matter. Many believe that it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the most natural type of dark matter to have \nformed in the early universe. That theoretical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convenience isn’t enough for us to accept \nCDM if it doesn’t explain our observations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out that despite hints to the \ncontrary, CDM may still be our best bet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The CDM simulations I’ve been talking \nabout so far are “dark-matter-only”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586080,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means that they don’t include ordinary \nmatter; they’re just simulating how dark matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591180,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behaves in the absence of gas and stars \nand all that visible stuff. Now that sounds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595980,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit forgetful on the part of the \nsimulators. But remember that there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than 5 times more dark matter than \nordinary matter in the universe. Almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604920,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the gravitational oomph in the universe is \nfrom dark matter, so it’s actually not crazy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610740,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for these simulations to ignore the relatively \npiddling contribution from atoms. But actually,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616860,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re now discovering that there are a couple of \nreasons why ordinary matter really does matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624660,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, ordinary matter is the stuff we actually \nsee, so when you’re trying to use a simulation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630600,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to predict what it is we should see, it’s helpful \nto have that stuff in your simulation; otherwise,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636480,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how do you predict what you’re going to \nsee through your telescope? For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640140,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we guessed that all of the dark matter \nsub-halos around a system like the Milky  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645600,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way should contain visible satellite galaxies. \nBut that turns out to be wrong. Sub-halos that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652620,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are less than around a billion times the mass \nof the sun are simply incapable of capturing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas from the intergalactic medium, because \nthe gas is too hot__its particles moving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663960,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too quickly—to become trapped in the shallow \ngravitational wells of these low-mass sub-halos.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670800,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So most of these sub-halos have few to no \nstars, and therefore we can’t actually see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676260,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them. If this is right then the satellites \naren’t missing, they’re just invisible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, our telescopes are becoming more and more \nsensitive and we’re starting to see a lot of very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689160,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faint galaxies and substructures, so we’re getting \ncloser to the predictions of the CDM models."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696420,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now the second reason that regular matter \nmatters. Although so-called “baryonic matter”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703440,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "represents only a small fraction of the mass in \nthe universe, this stuff is way more interesting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708540,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than dark matter because it can form us for example, but it can also influence dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714540,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter in unexpected ways. Because it can create \nstars, it can also produce supernova explosions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721920,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the most massive of those stars die. Every \ntime a supernova goes off, it blows away a huge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728520,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chunk of the gas surrounding it. That gas can \nbe forced out of the galactic core, or even out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the galaxy entirely. And this outgoing gas \nalso drags a little bit of dark matter with it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740160,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The dense galactic core of a young galaxy will \nexperience burst after burst of star formation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746820,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which leads to multiple waves of supernovae. \nOver time, quite a bit of dark matter can get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754020,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dragged gravitationally out of the core of the galaxy. This can ultimately flatten out what started as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760980,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a very high density core, eliminating \nthe cusps predicted by the CDM model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766260,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But CDM isn’t totally out of the woods yet. In \nrecent recent years, we’ve found some counter examples  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772500,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that seem to defy these needs fixes for CDM’s \nproblems. For example, there are several galaxies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778560,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that have the typical low density cores but don’t \nseem to have had enough star formation in their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783660,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pasts to do that supernova-driven smearing. And \nstrangely, also some systems with lots of star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790860,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation that actually have high-density cusps. \nLike for some reason the dark matter ignored the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796260,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernovae in these cases. So what started out as a “cusp-core” problem has actually evolved into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803940,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what’s being called a “density-diversity” problem. \nThere’s a very wide range of central densities in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810060,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies that doesn’t appear to be correlated with \nstar formation or the mass of the surrounding dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816240,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter halo. Currently it’s hard to see how the \nCDM model can accommodate this kind of diversity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The missing satellites solution also has a new \nproblem. While it’s true that small satellites  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829560,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are going to be invisible due to not holding onto \nenough gas, those CDM simulations do predict a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reasonable number of larger sub-haloes within a \ngalaxy, with masses more than a billion times that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844800,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the sun. These things should have no problem \nholding onto enough gas to make stars, so we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851220,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be able to see them. But we don’t really \n— at least not in the numbers predicted by CDM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856500,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simulations. So the “missing satellites” problem \nhas evolved into what’s known as “too-big-to-fail”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862860,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem: we expected to not see the smaller \nsubhaloes, but we still seem to be missing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869400,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of the bigger CDM halos—the ones that should be too massive to have failed to form galaxies and stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876960,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both the “density-diversity” and “too-big-to-fail” \nproblems are challenges to the CDM model because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883320,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’re difficult to solve by including \nregular matter. But they aren’t quite the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888480,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothesis-killers for CDM that the cusp-core and missing satellite problems once seemed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894780,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be. It may be that improved simulations \nand observations will solve these too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900720,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the prospects of CDM recovered, alternative \nmodels like warm or fuzzy dark matter are coming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907020,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under strain. Our improving telescope sensitivity \nis finding more and more small-scale structures  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913080,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that conflict with the predictions of WDM and \nFDM, and remember those were are all about stopping small-scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920280,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure. Now there are ways to tweak these models to better match our observations, but that kind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926460,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of defeats their original purpose, so they're \nperhaps not as parsimonious as they once were."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932340,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The bottom line is that there is still \na lot of uncertainty and debate about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both the microscopic constituents and the \nmacroscopic behavior of dark matter. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943320,
    "dDurationMs": 6766,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one thing is clear: ordinary matter is not nearly \nas insignificant to cosmology as we once thought.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950086,
    "dDurationMs": 4754,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order for us to finally solve one of the \ngreatest mysteries in all physics—in order  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954840,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to figure out the nature of dark matter, we’ll \nneed to continue to improve our understanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959460,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the complex ways in which all types of \nmatter shape the structures in Space Time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
